592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1031 "Introduction to Integration by Parts" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1033 317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1034 1.4476087343369344 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7280f087-059d-5524-8f8b-a1fe79b4ef9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428955 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1031 "Convergence of Fourier Series" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1033 3198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7742668d-ad15-5c25-bc82-4ea5b4fd800d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186220816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186220816 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186220816 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186220816 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186220816 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3fefa1eb-6b94-56ab-91b1-2a7b190c1a7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221196 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221196 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in [-L,L)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb R.$ \nGiven that $L=2$ and $f(x) = \\cosh{(x+1)}$, find the $4$th cosine and the $4$th sine coefficients." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221196 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8658f47-7f42-530a-a62e-1cc3144059aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221196 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k \\neq 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\dfrac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{L} = 2\\pi.$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\na_n\\cos\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)+\nb_n\\sin\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4$th cosine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, $4$th sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=2$ and $f(x)=\\cosh(x+1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x, \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221197 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221197 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3x^2-4x+1$ for $x\\in[-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221197 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba1cc59e-66ed-58fc-a494-b949c7ed0a88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221197 #edb/ref 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=3x^2-4x+1$ for $x \\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(({2}^{3}-2\\cdot {2}^{2}+2)-((-2)^{3}-2\\cdot (-2)^{2}+(-2))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-(-18)) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221198 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221198 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x+4$ for $x\\in[-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221198 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "823fc00b-48af-5e79-9dcc-5e519e81cd4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221198 #edb/ref 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=-5x+4$ for $x \\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_3 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2} (-5x+4)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-5x+4\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(uv\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[(-5x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\cdot (-5)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }[(-5(2)+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-(-5(-2)+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi )]+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot 0+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3\\pi }){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{20}{9{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3\\pi )]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{9{\\pi }^{2}}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221443 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221443 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186222018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186222028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221443 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Effects of the Horizontal Scaling on Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221443 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186221196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221443 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "12ff3452-19b6-566a-8b39-ad880dee8858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186222022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186222029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Constant Term For the Fourier Series" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186221197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221444 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cb301102-1a82-5a81-bb11-b9fc56cd1dc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186222023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186222024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Coefficients For the Fourier Series" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186221198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221445 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c3315887-ff5c-5fd7-93bd-a58800e378b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221473 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221473 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221473 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221473 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221473 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221473 #edb/ref 1045 "Fourier Series of Arbitrary Period" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221473 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186222376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221473 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221473 #edb/ref 1050 1260.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221473 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "80e6356e-9b75-58b6-9da9-f8c9047c131d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222023 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{5}.$\n\n${b}_{5}=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222023 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8c14d18-5531-531f-b86c-3260af1f5733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222023 #edb/ref 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{5}=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(6x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{3}(uv\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }{[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-{[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}+6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-((6(3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi )-(6(-3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5\\pi ))+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(36+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222376 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222376 #edb/ref 1061 601031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222376 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0045b15e-7a1f-5142-94c5-082ec814b34d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222376 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222377 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222377 #edb/ref 1061 601032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222377 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b4fe87b-2102-5820-bc57-996b33b5332d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222377 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222378 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222378 #edb/ref 1061 601035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222378 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1a6a016-3b6c-50d2-9290-1aa451df4a51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222378 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222379 #edb/ref 1061 601038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222379 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7ee00e8-fdc1-5440-bb38-f920d76832a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222379 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222514 #edb/ref 1064 "When a function has period $2L$ (instead of $2\\pi$), the \"usual\" Fourier formulas still work, but the sine and cosine inputs must be rescaled so that each term completes an integer number of oscillations over one period. \n\nWe do this by converting the $2L$-periodic function into a $2\\pi$-periodic one using a change of variables.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in[-L,L)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\nTo use the standard $2\\pi$-period Fourier formulas, define\n\n$$\ns=\\frac{\\pi}{L}x\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ng(s)=f\\!\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi}s\\right).\n$$\n\nThen $g$ is $2\\pi$-periodic.\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $g$, the Fourier series is\n\n$$\ng(s)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\bigl(a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns)\\bigr),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,ds, \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1, \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $g(s)=f\\!\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)$ and $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$.\nFirst, for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)cos\\,(n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,d\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCoefficients $b_n$ (for $n \\geq 1$) and $a_0$ can be found similarly.\n\nTo summarize, the Fourier series of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(a_n\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,dx \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1